course: world
lesson: 28
title: Migration and hibernation
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Winter is hard for animals: it is cold, days are short, and food like insects, fruit and fresh leaves becomes scarce, though seeds and nuts can still be found.
explain: Some animals migrate: they make a long journey to a place with more food and better weather, and come back later; swallows fly from Europe to Africa each autumn and return in spring.
explain: Some animals hibernate: they go into a deep sleep in a sheltered place, their body cools down, their heart beats slowly, and they live on fat stored in autumn.
explain: Others stay active and change: they grow thicker fur, store food like squirrels do, or change colour, like the Arctic hare that turns white for the snow.
explain: A hedgehog curled up in a pile of leaves in winter may breathe only a few times a minute; it is not just sleeping, its whole body has slowed down to save energy.
fact: Migration is a long journey animals make to find food, warmth or a place to have young.
fact: Many birds, such as swallows and storks, migrate south in autumn and return in spring.
fact: Migrating birds find their way using the Sun, the stars, landmarks and the Earth's magnetism.
fact: The Arctic tern makes one of the longest migrations, flying from the Arctic to the Antarctic and back every year.
fact: Whales, salmon, wildebeest and some butterflies also migrate.
fact: Hibernation is a long, deep winter sleep in which the body temperature drops and the heart beats slowly.
fact: Hedgehogs, dormice, many bats and ground squirrels hibernate.
fact: Before hibernating, animals eat a lot to store fat, which keeps them alive through the winter.
fact: Waking up in the middle of hibernation uses up a lot of the stored fat, so a hibernating animal should never be disturbed.
fact: Bears sleep for months in winter, but their body cools only a little, so many scientists call it a lighter winter sleep.
fact: Frogs and many reptiles become very slow and still in winter because they cannot keep themselves warm.
fact: Some animals stay active in winter by storing food, growing thicker fur or changing colour.
exercise: What is migration? | A long journey to find food or warmth | A deep sleep through the winter | Changing colour to match the snow | Storing nuts and seeds for the cold months
exercise: What is hibernation? | A deep winter sleep with a slowed body | A long flight to a warmer land in the south | Building a nest for the young | Growing new feathers in spring
exercise: Why do swallows leave Europe in autumn? | The insects they eat become scarce | They do not like the colour of snow | They are chased out by other birds | Their nests fall apart in the rain
exercise: Which of these animals hibernates? | A hedgehog | A swallow | A stork | A wildebeest
exercise: What keeps a hibernating animal alive through the winter? | Fat stored in its body in autumn | Food it wakes up to eat each day | Warm air from the Sun in its den | Water it drinks from the snow
exercise: Why do animals eat a lot in autumn before hibernating? | To store fat for the winter | To grow taller before spring | To keep their teeth sharp | To make their fur change colour
exercise: What happens to a hibernating animal's body? | It cools down and its heart slows | It heats up and its heart races | It grows bigger and stronger | It stops needing any energy at all
exercise: How do migrating birds find their way? | Using the Sun, stars, landmarks and magnetism | By following other kinds of animals only | By smelling the warm air far away | By flying wherever the wind happens to blow them
exercise: Which bird makes one of the longest migrations? | The Arctic tern | The house sparrow | The pigeon | The chicken
exercise: Which of these animals also migrates? | Salmon | Hedgehog | Dormouse | Earthworm
exercise: How does an Arctic hare survive winter without migrating? | It turns white to hide in the snow | It flies south to warmer lands | It sleeps for the whole winter | It swims under the ice all winter
exercise: How do squirrels prepare for winter? | They store nuts and seeds | They fly to a warmer place | They grow thick feathers | They shed all their fur
exercise: Why do frogs become slow and still in winter? | They cannot keep themselves warm | They are too full from autumn food | They are hiding from the swallows | They are waiting to lay their eggs
exercise: Why do insect-eating bats hibernate? | There are almost no insects in winter | Their wings freeze in the cold | They cannot see in the dark winter | They are too heavy to fly in winter
test: A storks' nest on a roof stands empty all winter and the storks return in April. What did the storks do? | They migrated | They hibernated | They changed colour | They stored food
test: A child wakes a hibernating hedgehog in January. Why is this harmful? | Waking uses up fat it needs for winter | The hedgehog will grow much too fast and too big | The hedgehog will forget its home | It will start to migrate south
test: Why does a dormouse not need to eat for months in winter? | Its slowed body uses little energy from its fat | It eats the snow around its nest | It has a big stomach that it filled with food in autumn | It catches insects in its sleep
test: Which animal stays active all winter? | A squirrel with a store of nuts | A hibernating dormouse in its nest | A swallow spending winter in Africa | A bat sleeping deeply in a dark cave
test: Why would a bird that eats seeds be more likely to stay for winter than one that eats flying insects? | Seeds can still be found in winter | Seed-eaters cannot fly very far | Seeds keep birds warm inside | Insect-eaters like warm sand
test: Which is true about bears in winter? | Their body cools only a little while they sleep | They fly to warmer lands each year | They turn white like Arctic hares | They eat one large meal every day and then go back to sleep
test: Wildebeest walk hundreds of kilometres across Africa each year following the rains. Why? | To find fresh grass and water | To escape the cold winter snow | To find a cave to hibernate in | To change the colour of their fur
test: A bat hangs very still in a cold cave all winter, with a slow heartbeat. What is it doing? | Hibernating | Migrating south | Hunting for moths | Building a nest
test: Why do many migrating birds come back north in spring? | There is plenty of food for raising young | The south becomes covered in snow | They are blown back by winter winds | They need to hibernate in the north
test: why do swallows fly south in autumn | To find food and warmth for the winter | To lay their eggs in the snow | To get away from the long days | Because the wind blows them there
test: what's hibernating mean | A long deep winter sleep with the body cooling down | Flying south for the winter | Changing colour in the snow | Storing lots of food in a burrow to eat in the winter
test: Tom says bears hibernate just like hedgehogs. true? | No, a bear's body cools only a little; it's a lighter winter sleep | Yes, bears cool down just as much | Yes, bears are the deepest sleepers of all, much deeper than hedgehogs | No, bears stay awake all winter
test: Mia reckons hibernating animals wake up to eat every day. right? | No, they live on the fat they stored before | Yes, they eat a meal every day | Yes, they wake up for breakfast each morning | No, they don't need any food or fat at all
exam: Which of the following is designated by the term migration? | A long journey undertaken in search of food, warmth or a place to rear young | A deep winter sleep with lowered body temperature | A change of colour to match the season | The storage of food against the winter
exam: Identify, from among the following, a bird celebrated for one of the longest annual migrations. | The Arctic tern | The ostrich | The penguin | The house sparrow
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the preparation of animals for hibernation. | They consume much food and store it as fat | They cease eating entirely in the autumn | They migrate to warmer lands | They grow additional feathers
exam: Identify the principal reason for which frogs become slow and inactive in winter. | They are unable to maintain their own bodily warmth | They are migrating beneath the ice | They have consumed excessive food | They are protecting their eggs
